<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xavier Coppolani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xavier Coppolani was a Corsican-born French colonial administrator and scholar of Islamic brotherhoods. From 1902 he pursued a policy of peaceful penetration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, using alliances with religious leaders to bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of Trarza and Brakna under French protection, until he was assassinated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tijikja">Tidjikja</a> in 1905.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xavier-coppolani.jpg" alt="Xavier Coppolani" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agence Rol · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xavier_Coppolani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Xavier Coppolani was born on 2 February 1866 at Marignana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>. His father, Dominique, was counted among the colons, the French settlers of French Algeria. In Algeria Coppolani grew up among local Muslims and learnt Algerian Arabic, and he became intrigued by Islamic practice and studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1899 he was transferred to French Sudan to lead the expansion of colonial rule north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a>, where Moorish tribes held firm against the French. The mission was presented as an inquiry, but its aim was the subjugation of the local people. Using local tribal rivalries, in 1901 he drew up a plan for moving into the territory that combined military and political methods.</p><p>He made alliances with two leading marabouts of the Qadiriyya Sufi brotherhoods, Shaykh Sidya Baba and Shaykh Saad Bouh, promising them a dominant role in the colonial administration and protection for their Zawiya tribes against the attacks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hassan">Hassane</a> warriors. In return they used their religious influence to persuade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> to accept French rule. Backed by military pressure, the plan worked, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of Tagant, Trarza and Brakna accepted French rule in 1903-04.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Adrar resisted, backed by a third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a> marabout, Shaykh Ma al-'Aynayn, who was in turn supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Coppolani was preparing to march on Adrar when he was killed on 12 May 1905 by a member of the shaykh's Gudfiyya brotherhood. General Gouraud defeated Adrar and incorporated it by force into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> in 1912, but tribal revolts and raids went on until 1934. Coppolani's work was instrumental in the colonial occupation and creation of modern Mauritania.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xavier Coppolani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1903 France, under <b>Xavier Coppolani's</b> policy of peaceful penetration, made Brakna a protectorate, and it became part of French Mauritania.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-brakna">Emirate of Brakna</a></li><li><blockquote>France's <b>Xavier Coppolani</b> occupied Tijikja in 1905 and was assassinated there the same year.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-tagant">Emirate of Tagant</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1902 <b>Xavier Coppolani</b> pursued a policy of peaceful penetration, using alliances with religious leaders to bring the Emirate of Trarza and the Emirate of Brakna under French protection in 1903.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-mauritania">French Mauritania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Marignana, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tijikja">Tidjikja</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.23,8.8&amp;year=1891" aria-label="Marignana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Xavier Coppolani"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5555 3940h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5555 3940h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.23,8.8&amp;year=1891">What was here in 1891? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
